          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"stael-corinne-3cff302d84f1b4da\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":0,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.98,\"analysis\":\"Corinne’s embedded “je vous aime” directly avows as true her continuing love for Oswald. It occurs within a reflective explanation of how painfully and comprehensively that love dominates her life. The wording neither undertakes a relational commitment nor functions as a reflex-like affective discharge.\",\"ambiguity\":\"The phrase is grammatically embedded in “d’aimer comme je vous aime” rather than offered as a standalone declaration, but its avowal of love is unequivocal.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"Corinne speaks the embedded equivalent “je vous aime” directly to Oswald within the clause “cela me fait mal… d’aimer comme je vous aime.”\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"Corinne confirms her love while explaining that its dominance threatens the talents, imagination, and public life on which she depends. Her attempt to regain independence by attending the ball does not retract the avowal. The exchange foregrounds the absence of a settled shared future: Corinne says they are not destined for one another, while Oswald remains unwilling to articulate the practical design his feelings would entail. This supports T but actively weakens P; the emotional pain and intensity do not independently establish E.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“Non, répondit Corinne… cela me fait mal, et beaucoup de mal, d’aimer comme je vous aime.”\",\"supports\":\"Corinne explicitly rejects the suggestion that she wishes to cease loving Oswald and presents her present love as a painful fact, strongly supporting T.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“le sentiment que vous m’inspirez doit absorber tout autre intérêt et toute autre idée”\",\"supports\":\"Her surrounding explanation characterizes love as a state that absorbs her other interests, reinforcing the truth-conditional avowal rather than an undertaking.\",\"confidence\":0.97},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“puisque nous ne sommes point destinés l’un à l’autre”\",\"supports\":\"Corinne explicitly frames the pair as lacking a shared destiny, providing evidence against commitment or relational-undertaking force in the core utterance.\",\"confidence\":0.97},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Lord Nelvil remains silent because expressing his feeling would require stating the plan it inspired, which his heart does not yet know.\",\"supports\":\"The narration distinguishes emotional avowal from practical intention or commitment, supporting P=0 for Corinne’s highlighted avowal.\",\"confidence\":0.95}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T naturally captures the core force as a reflective avowal of an existing loving state. The surrounding conflict and emotional intensity require contextual explanation but create no residual pressure beyond T/P/E.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":null}"
        }
      ],
